  <header class="page">
    <p class="crumb"><a href="{{ '/projects/' | relative_url }}">Projects</a></p>
    <h1>Place &amp; Mental Health</h1>
    <p class="dek">Two studies replacing single-number exposures with data-driven typologies: trajectories of addictive screen use in 4,285 adolescents, and social determinant clusters across 3,018 United States counties.</p>
  </header>

  <figure class="fig-full">
    <div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/place-mental-health/jama_trajectories.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/place-mental-health/jama_trajectories.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Three panels showing latent class trajectories of addictive social media, mobile phone, and video game use from age eleven to fifteen" width="2400" height="784" loading="eager" decoding="async"></a></div>
    <figcaption><b>The shape of exposure, not its size.</b> Latent class trajectories of addictive social media, mobile phone, and video game use from age eleven to fifteen. Nearly one-third of participants followed an increasing trajectory for social media or mobile phones. From Xiao, <b>Meng, Y.</b> (co-first author) et al., <i>JAMA</i> (2025).</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section id="question">
    <p class="eyebrow">Research question</p>
    <h2 class="sec">Which environments accumulate into mental health risk?</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Screen time and area deprivation are both normally entered into a model as a single number. That compresses a multidimensional environment into one dimension, and it discards the shape of exposure over time. A child who uses social media heavily throughout adolescence and one whose use climbs steeply from age eleven can have the same average; a rural county with old housing and a dense county with unequal income can score alike on a deprivation index while presenting entirely different risks.</p>
      <p>Both studies here replace that single number with a data-driven typology, then ask what the typology predicts. In each case the pattern carries risk that the level does not, and the risk falls unevenly across sex, age, and race and ethnicity. The two studies work at opposite ends of the scale, one following individuals through four years of adolescence and one following counties across a decade, but they make the same methodological argument.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="approach">
    <p class="eyebrow">Approach</p>
    <h2 class="sec">Three moves across two studies</h2>
    <div class="cols3">
      <div class="step"><h3>Replace the measure with a typology</h3><p>Latent class linear mixed models recover trajectories of addictive use. Unsupervised clustering recovers county types from 284 social determinant variables. Neither compresses the environment into a single ordered score.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>Let exposure accumulate</h3><p>Exposure is measured repeatedly, across four years of adolescence and across a decade of county history, so that the trajectory rather than the level enters the model as the predictor.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>Estimate the disparity, not the average</h3><p>Risk ratios by trajectory and incidence rate ratios by cluster are reported for sex, age, and race and ethnicity subgroups, because the purpose of a typology is that the groups within it differ.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="digital">
    <p class="eyebrow">Theme 1</p>
    <h2 class="sec">The environment a child grows up inside</h2>

    <div class="study" id="s-jama">
      <p class="venue"><span class="j">JAMA</span> &middot; 2025 &middot; co-first author</p>
      <h3>Addictive use trajectories, not total screen time</h3>
      <p class="cite">Xiao, Y., <b>Meng, Y.</b> (co-first author), Brown, T. T., Keyes, K. M., &amp; Mann, J. J. (2025). Addictive screen use trajectories and suicidal behaviors, suicidal ideation, and mental health in US youths. <i>JAMA</i>. doi:10.1001/jama.2025.7829</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Data from the Adolescent Brain Cognitive Development Study, covering 2016 to 2022 across twenty-one United States sites, were used to identify trajectories of addictive use of social media, mobile phones, and video games from validated child-reported measures at the year 2, year 3, and year 4 follow-ups. The analytic sample was 4,285 youths with a mean baseline age of 10.0 years. Latent class linear mixed models recovered three trajectories for social media and for mobile phones and two for video games. Nearly one-third of participants followed an increasing addictive use trajectory for social media or mobile phones beginning at age eleven.</p>
        <p>In adjusted models, an increasing addictive use trajectory for social media carried a risk ratio of 2.14 (95% CI, 1.61 to 2.85) for suicidal behaviors relative to the low trajectory, and a high-peaking trajectory carried 2.39 (1.66 to 3.43). High trajectories were associated with suicide-related outcomes for every screen type. Baseline total screen time, entered on its own, was not associated with suicidal behaviors, suicidal ideation, or internalizing or externalizing symptoms. The pattern of use over time carries the risk that the amount of use does not, which is why a cross-sectional measure of hours per day has produced such an inconsistent literature.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig-full" style="margin:18px 0 20px">
        <div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/place-mental-health/jama_social-media-associations.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/place-mental-health/jama_social-media-associations.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Forest plot of unadjusted and adjusted risk ratios and mean differences for suicidal behaviors, suicidal ideation, and internalizing and externalizing symptoms by social media addictive use trajectory" width="2400" height="792" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
        <figcaption><b>Social media trajectories against year 4 outcomes.</b> Unadjusted and adjusted risk ratios and mean differences for suicidal behaviors, suicidal ideation, and internalizing and externalizing symptoms, with the low trajectory as reference.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="links"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2025.7829">DOI</a><a href="{{ '/assets/pdf/projects/xiaoAddictiveScreenUse2025.pdf' | relative_url }}">PDF</a><a href="{{ '/assets/bib/xiaoAddictiveScreenUse2025.bib' | relative_url }}" download>Cite (BibTeX)</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="county">
    <p class="eyebrow">Theme 2</p>
    <h2 class="sec">The environment a county provides</h2>

    <div class="study" id="s-nmh">
      <p class="venue"><span class="j">Nature Mental Health</span> &middot; 2025 &middot; co-first author</p>
      <h3>Social determinants as county types rather than an index</h3>
      <p class="cite">Xiao, Y., <b>Meng, Y.</b> (co-first author), Brown, T. T., Tsai, A. C., Snowden, L. R., Chow, J. C.-C., Pathak, J., &amp; Mann, J. J. (2025). Machine learning to investigate policy-relevant social determinants of health and suicide rates in the United States. <i>Nature Mental Health</i>, 1–10.</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Aggregated social determinant data across 3,018 United States counties for 2009, 2014, and 2019 were linked to county suicide rates from the National Vital Statistics System. Unsupervised clustering over 284 variables identified three county types rather than a ranking. REMOTE counties are rural and elderly, with marginalized environments, old housing, traditional systems, and empty houses. COPE counties combine complex family dynamics, high consumption of health services, poverty, and extreme heat. DIVERSE counties are dense, immigrant-rich, environmentally challenged, economically unequal, racially and ethnically diverse, with saturated health care and expensive housing.</p>
        <p>Negative binomial regression related the clusters to suicide rates. REMOTE counties showed higher overall rates, particularly among men. COPE counties showed elevated rates among white populations. DIVERSE counties showed increased rates among women and among Black and Hispanic populations. The distribution of suicide rates across states tracked the distribution of clusters within those states. A one-dimensional deprivation index cannot produce this result, because the three types are not ordered on a single axis: each is disadvantaged in a different way, and each concentrates risk in a different population.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig-hug" style="margin:18px 0 20px">
        <div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/place-mental-health/nmh_chord-clusters.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/place-mental-health/nmh_chord-clusters.jpg' | relative_url }}" style="max-height:460px" alt="Chord diagrams for 2009, 2014, and 2019 showing how main social determinant domains and sub-domains distribute across the three county clusters" width="2400" height="1741" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
        <figcaption><b>What separates the three county types.</b> Chord diagrams for 2009, 2014, and 2019 showing how the main social determinant domains, and then their sub-domains, distribute across REMOTE, COPE, and DIVERSE counties. Wider chords indicate more unfavorable values.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="fig-full" style="margin:0 0 20px">
        <div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/place-mental-health/nmh_suicide-rates.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/place-mental-health/nmh_suicide-rates.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Panels of county suicide rates by demographic subgroup within each cluster, for 2008 to 2010, 2013 to 2015, and 2018 to 2020" width="2400" height="1057" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
        <figcaption>Suicide rates by subgroup within each cluster, across three periods. The cluster differences persist across a decade rather than reflecting one year's data.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="fig-full">
        <div class="plate"><a class="figure-link" href="{{ '/assets/img/projects/place-mental-health/nmh_coefficients.jpg' | relative_url }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="{{ '/assets/img/projects/place-mental-health/nmh_coefficients.jpg' | relative_url }}" alt="Coefficient plot with 95 percent confidence intervals from the negative binomial model relating county clusters to suicide rates, by subgroup and period" width="2400" height="1063" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a></div>
        <figcaption>Incidence rate ratios with 95 percent intervals, each cluster against the others, by subgroup and period. The reversals between subgroups are the finding an index would average away.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="links"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s44220-025-00424-4">DOI</a><a href="{{ '/assets/pdf/projects/xiaoMachineLearningInvestigate2025.pdf' | relative_url }}">PDF</a><a href="{{ '/assets/bib/xiaoMachineLearningInvestigate2025.bib' | relative_url }}" download>Cite (BibTeX)</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="leads">
    <p class="eyebrow">Continuity</p>
    <h2 class="sec">Where this leads</h2>
    <div class="leads">
      <p>This is where the research program moves from measuring places to measuring the people in them. Both studies replace a single exposure measure with a data-driven typology and then estimate disparities within it rather than an average across it, and that design is what the <a href="{{ '/projects/place-cognitive-health/' | relative_url }}">cognitive health work</a> inherits. The county clusters are built from the same class of social and environmental variables that the <a href="{{ '/projects/sensing-place-function/' | relative_url }}">sensing work</a> produces at far finer resolution, which is the route to estimating a social determinant typology below the county level, where the exposure a person actually experiences is defined.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

